          "tool_use_id": "toolu_015htGwgNSznzGCGD28Z1Tbu",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022concepts/bloom-filter.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Bloom Filter\\ndescription: A probabilistic set-membership data structure using a bit array and hash functions to test membership with tunable false-positive rates.\\ntimestamp: 2026-01-05T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Probabilistic-Data-Structure\\n  - concept/Set-Membership\\n  - tech/Algorithms\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Single source providing core definition and characteristics; no corroboration yet.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\nA **bloom filter** is a probabilistic set-membership data structure that answers membership queries with different certainty levels:\\n\\n- **\\u0022Definitely not present\\u0022** \\u2014 answered with certainty\\n- **\\u0022Possibly present\\u0022** \\u2014 answered with a tunable false-positive rate\\n\\n## Implementation\\n\\nA bloom filter uses:\\n\\n- A **bit array** as its underlying storage\\n- **k independent hash functions** to determine bit positions\\n\\nWhen adding an element, the k hash functions are applied to produce k bit positions, and each corresponding bit in the array is set to 1. When querying membership, the same hash functions are applied; if any of the resulting bits is 0, the element is definitely not in the set. If all bits are 1, the element is possibly in the set (a false positive is possible).\\n\\n## Key Constraints\\n\\n**Cannot enumerate members**: A bloom filter does not store elements themselves, only membership markers, so its contents cannot be enumerated.\\n\\n**Cannot support deletion in basic form**: Removing a set bit to delete an element risks breaking membership tests for other elements that happen to hash to the same bit. Deleting one element could incorrectly report that other, unrelated elements are no longer present.\\n\\nSome variants (e.g. counting bloom filters) address the deletion limitation at the cost of additional storage or complexity.\\n\\n## Trade-offs\\n\\nThe tunable false-positive rate and compact memory footprint make bloom filters useful for:\\n- Quick negative checks (avoiding expensive lookups)\\n- Space-efficient approximate membership testing\\n- Cache filtering and network packet inspection\\n\\nHowever, the impossibility of accurate deletion and enumeration limits their use in scenarios where these operations are required.\\n\u0022}"
        }
